<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biedermeier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biedermeier is the name given to the middle-class culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 and the revolutions of 1848. Under censorship and political repression, it turned to private and domestic life, producing simple, comfortable furniture, intimate genre painting, house music and sentimental literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biedermeier.jpg" alt="Biedermeier" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Spitzweg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Spitzweg_-_Sonntagsspaziergang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Biedermeier period was an era in the art and culture of the German Confederation between 1815 and 1848, in which the middle classes grew and artists produced works that appealed to them. It began with the end of the Napoleonic Wars and ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a>. Two forces drove it: growing urbanisation and industrialisation, which created a new urban middle class and a new audience for the arts, and the political stability under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>. Artists and society concentrated on the domestic and, at least in public, the non-political.</p><p>The name comes from the fictional mediocre poet Gottlieb Biedermaier, invented by the country doctor Adolf Kussmaul and the lawyer Ludwig Eichrodt for poems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> satirical weekly Fliegende Blätter in 1850. The verses parodied the depoliticised, petit-bourgeois people of the era. Bieder means plain, and Maier is a common surname. The word has been used as a label for the epoch since around 1900.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The values associated with Biedermeier include affection, sensibility, moderation and modesty, and Gemütlichkeit, a state of cosiness and friendliness. Because of strict control of publication and official censorship, writers turned to non-political subjects such as historical fiction and country life, and political talk stayed at home among close friends. Typical poets were Annette von Droste-Hülshoff, Adelbert von Chamisso, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-morike">Eduard Mörike</a> and Wilhelm Müller, and Adalbert Stifter wrote the novel Der Nachsommer. Painting preferred the everyday world with few grand gestures, as in Friedrich von Amerling's Portrait of the Arthaber Family of 1837 and the landscapes of Ferdinand Georg Waldmüller. Carl Spitzweg was another key painter.</p><p>Biedermeier furniture was the first style to arise from the growing middle class. A simplified reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> style, it used local woods such as cherry, ash and oak rather than imported mahogany. Pieces from 1815 to 1830 were the most severe and neoclassical, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> produced unique designs. In music, publications of operatic excerpts, Lieder and symphonic arrangements allowed amateurs to play at home on the piano.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>An exhibition at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> applied arts museum in 1896 brought the style new admirers and a revival among European cabinetmakers that lasted until Art Deco. In Wilhelmine Germany, social reformers saw Biedermeier architecture as a model of middle-class domestic reform, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> architects such as Paul Schultze-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a> and Paul Mebes promoted a neo-Biedermeier style for new housing.</p><p>In the Czech lands the period coincided with the Czech National Revival, with writers such as Božena Němcová and Karel Hynek Mácha and painters such as Josef Navrátil. The Polish architectural style Świdermajer was named as a play on the word. The Geymüllerschlössel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, built in 1808, now houses the Biedermeier collection of the Museum of Applied Arts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Biedermeier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Public life under Metternich was constrained by censorship, so the comfortable private culture of the <b>Biedermeier</b> middle classes flourished instead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
